% --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{abstract}
\noindent
This report answers the three questions of Assignment~3 with fully reproducible
experiments. For Question~1 we design 20 counterfactual audit items covering ten
dimensions of potential bias --- including Global South representation and two
dimensions on caste, religion and religious accommodation --- and evaluate four open-weight instruction-tuned language
models with three independent samples per item (480 responses). A deterministic
annotation pipeline records refusal, sentiment, favourability, specificity, length and
curated stereotype/agency/accommodation lexicons, and a pooled-standard-deviation
composite quantifies disparity on a single scale. We find mean composite disparities of
0.28--0.52 pooled standard deviations, increasing as model size decreases within the
Qwen2.5 family, but the effect is concentrated in response \emph{substance} (length,
specificity, favourability of decisions) rather than in sentiment: signed sentiment
differences are indistinguishable from zero and surface stereotype and refusal rates are
essentially nil. Only 7 of 20 pre-specified directional hypotheses are supported per
model, and several large effects run opposite to the expected stereotype direction.
Two inference-time mitigations (a fairness system prompt and a counterfactual
self-audit rewrite) do not reduce disparity reliably; one model becomes measurably
worse. For Question~2 we implement FedAvg from scratch for a 0.42M-parameter CNN on a
balanced 12{,}000/2{,}000 Fashion-MNIST subset with five clients. Under an IID partition
FedAvg reaches 0.885 test accuracy against 0.889 for centralized training (0.4-point
gap), while Dirichlet($\alpha{=}0.1$) and a two-class-per-client label skew widen the gap
to 5.8 and 27.8 points and produce strongly uneven per-client accuracy. For Question~3
we implement DP-SGD with per-example gradients, clipping and calibrated Gaussian noise,
and audit it with loss/confidence/entropy threshold attacks and a four-shadow-model
logistic attack. At $\sigma{=}0.8$ ($\eps{=}6.5$ RDP, $\delta{=}10^{-5}$) accuracy is
0.812 at $C{=}1$ and 0.748 at $C{=}5$; at $\sigma{=}1.2$ it falls to 0.795 and 0.636.
Membership-inference AUC drops from 0.560 for the non-private baseline (0.579 for a
deliberately overfit baseline) to 0.500--0.518 for all DP configurations. We discuss what
these numbers do and do not establish, including the limits of small-scale auditing and
of empirical privacy evaluation.
\end{abstract}

