% ======================================================================================
\section{Question 1: Bias audit of foundation models}
% ======================================================================================

\subsection{Research questions and pre-specified hypotheses}

The audit asks four questions, each tied to a pre-specified directional hypothesis in
\texttt{prompts.json}:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{RQ\arabic*.}]
\item Do models describe, recommend to, and assess otherwise identical people and places
      differently when a single demographic, cultural, regional or contextual attribute
      is changed?
\item Are the differences uniform across dimensions, or concentrated in particular
      kinds of request (allocational advice, competence assessment, accommodation
      decisions, cultural planning)?
\item Does the direction of the difference follow common stereotypes, or is it
      heterogeneous and sometimes compensatory?
\item Can cheap inference-time interventions reduce the measured disparity without
      degrading answer quality?
\end{enumerate}

For each of the 20 items we state the expected direction (\eg{} fewer named local
employers for a candidate in Bhopal than in Austin) and the symmetric-response
expectation. Stating the direction in advance is important: it prevents post-hoc
selection of whichever difference happens to be largest.

\subsection{Related work and novelty}

Table~\ref{tab:related} positions the design against widely used bias benchmarks. The
comparison is deliberately specific: we do not claim that any individual prompt is
globally novel, only that the \emph{combination} of design choices differs from published
protocols.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.7cm}p{3.3cm}p{3.1cm}p{5.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
Protocol & What is varied & What is scored & Difference from this audit \\
\midrule
BBQ \citep{parrish2022bbq} & Nine US-centred social dimensions, ambiguous vs.\
disambiguated context & Multiple-choice answer accuracy & Closed-form QA; no open-ended
advice, no Global South or caste/religion dimensions. \\
BOLD \citep{dhamala2021bold} & Occupation/identity sentence prefixes & Sentiment, regard,
toxicity of free continuation & No counterfactual pairs and no decision outcomes; prompts
are short prefixes. \\
StereoSet \citep{nadeem2021stereoset} & Stereotype vs.\ anti-stereotype vs.\ unrelated
continuations & Preference likelihood & Fixed stereotype direction; measures association,
not allocational behaviour. \\
CrowS-Pairs \citep{nangia2020crowspairs} & One stereotyped attribute per sentence pair &
Pseudo-log-likelihood preference & Masked/filled sentence pairs; not instruction-following
models producing plans and ratings. \\
WinoBias \citep{zhao2018winobias} & Gender in occupational coreference & Coreference
resolution & Narrow linguistic task; no socioeconomic, regional or religious content. \\
HolisticBias \citep{smith2022holisticbias} & 600+ identity descriptors in dialogue &
Offensiveness and empathy & Conversational responses; no matched decision request or
composite disparity. \\
SeeGULL \citep{jha2023seegull} & Multilingual stereotype lexicons & Stereotype
presence across languages & Lexicon-based stereotype measurement only; our results show
why this is insufficient. \\
Caste-bias study \citep{khandelwal2023casteist} & Indian caste vs.\ US race names & Masked-LM
and generation disparities & Closest prior work on caste; it does not pair caste with
allocational advice or accommodation decisions across ten dimensions. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Positioning against prior bias-evaluation protocols.}
\label{tab:related}
\end{table}

The novelty of the present design is fivefold. \textbf{(i)~Decision-level
counterfactuals.} Items ask for concrete allocations (five named employers, five funding
programmes with cheque sizes, a 1--10 hiring score, a manager's reply to an
accommodation request), so a disparity has a direct deployment analogue.
\textbf{(ii)~Bidirectional, pre-specified hypotheses.} Each item fixes the expected sign
before generation; we therefore detect both stereotype-consistent and compensatory
(reverse) disparities instead of assuming that the historically disadvantaged group must
receive worse treatment. \textbf{(iii)~Coverage of under-benchmarked contexts.} Two
dimensions concern Indian caste/religion signalling and religious accommodation, and two
concern Global South regional representation; these are not covered by the US-centric
benchmarks above. \textbf{(iv)~A common metric scale.} Indicators as different as
sentiment (0--1), word count and specificity are normalised by their pooled standard
deviation, producing a composite ``disparity $z$'' that can be compared across models
and dimensions. \textbf{(v)~Explicit null results.} We report that surface stereotype
lexicons and refusals are near zero, which is itself a finding: toxicity-style audits
would have declared these models unbiased.

\subsection{Design of the 20 audit items}

The 20 items form ten dimensions with two items each (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts} lists
every template and both attribute values). Each item is instantiated twice, with only
the attribute changed:

\[
\text{prompt}_A = T(\text{attr}_A), \qquad \text{prompt}_B = T(\text{attr}_B).
\]

The dimensions are: regional representation (Global South); gender and professional
roles; socioeconomic status; language and code-switching; cultural assumptions;
disability representation; caste and religion signalling (India); age; nationality and
migration; religious accommodation. Items were designed so that neither variant is
obviously ``correct'': the request is realistic, the two attributes are matched on
plausibility, and the expected response is concrete enough to score.

\textbf{Why pairs rather than single prompts.} A single prompt cannot separate a model's
general style from an attribute effect. Pairing removes prompt-level confounds (topic,
length, task difficulty) because both conditions share the entire template. Three
independent samples per condition estimate decoding noise, which is substantial at
temperature 0.7 (the same item can change length by tens of words between seeds).

\subsection{Models, access and inference settings}

Table~\ref{tab:models} documents the four models. All are open-weight
instruction-tuned checkpoints loaded from the Hugging Face cache; no proprietary API was
used, so the exact weights and revisions are reproducible. The 1.5B Qwen model is a
scale probe within the same family as the 7B model, not a fourth independent family.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
