Each response record stores the model identifier, resolved commit hash, item and variant,
seed, decoding parameters, wall-clock time and the full text. This makes the audit
re-runnable and lets a reader recompute every statistic from the released JSONL.

\subsection{Annotation pipeline}

\texttt{q1\_score.py} applies the same deterministic annotator to every response. It
produces five families of indicators:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \textbf{Refusal}: whether the model declines, deflects or states it cannot help,
      detected with a curated phrase list.
\item \textbf{Sentiment}: mean sentence-level $P(\text{positive})$ from a fixed
      off-the-shelf DistilBERT SST-2 classifier, plus the polarity of the final sentence
      (the ``bottom line'').
\item \textbf{Favourability}: an item-specific parser that extracts the scalar the item
      asks for --- a 1--10 or 0--100 rating, a yes/no decision, a referral or feasibility
      label, an approval/denial of a request. Higher means more favourable to the person
      or place described.
\item \textbf{Specificity}: list items, named entities, numbers and concrete resources
      per 100 words.
\item \textbf{Curated lexicons}: stereotype/generalisation phrases, agency and communion
      words, accommodation, respect, condescension, discouragement, deficit, othering and
      risk language, each normalised per 100 words.
\end{enumerate}

The classifier was chosen because it is small, fixed and English-trained, matching the
English or transliterated-Hindi responses; it is applied identically to all conditions,
so any classifier bias is shared. Every lexicon is visible in the source file. The
annotator is transparent but shallow: it cannot recognise sarcasm or implicit
condescension that uses none of the listed phrases. We therefore treat it as one
imperfect instrument and cross-check the main conclusions against the item-level
favourability and length measures, which are parser-based and independent of the
sentiment model.

\textbf{Composite disparity index.} For response $r$ on item $i$, variant $v$ and seed
$s$, let $\Delta_{i,m} = \bar{x}_{i,B,m} - \bar{x}_{i,A,m}$ be the seed-averaged
difference for indicator $m$. The composite is
\[
z_i \;=\; \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}_i|}\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}_i}
        \frac{|\Delta_{i,m}|}{\hat{\sigma}_m},
\]
where $\hat{\sigma}_m$ is the pooled standard deviation of indicator $m$ over all
baseline responses and $\mathcal{M}_i$ is the set of indicators defined for item $i$.
Thus $z=1$ means an average difference of one pooled standard deviation across the
available indicators. The index uses absolute values because a signed average cancels
when a model is favourable to one group on some items and to the other on others; the
signed deltas are analysed separately.

\subsection{Statistical analysis}

The design is paired at the item level. For each model and indicator we compute the 20
item-level deltas (seed-averaged) and report:
\begin{itemize}
\item a cluster bootstrap over items (5{,}000 resamples) for the 95\% confidence interval
      of the mean delta;
\item a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the 20 item deltas, with Holm correction
      across the seven composite indicators;
\item a Friedman test across the four models on item-level composite $z$, with pairwise
      Wilcoxon tests and Holm correction;
\item the fraction of items whose delta sign matches the pre-specified direction, and the
      fraction whose sign is stable across all three seeds;
\item sign consistency across seeds (the proportion of item--seed triples that agree with
      the majority sign).
\end{itemize}

With 20 items the minimum achievable two-sided Wilcoxon $p$-value is about $2\times
10^{-6}$, but power for small effects is limited; this is a fundamental constraint of
small audits and is discussed in Section~\ref{sec:q1limits}. All tests are reported,
including non-significant ones, rather than selecting a metric after inspection.

\subsection{Results}

\paragraph{Model-level disparity.} Table~\ref{tab:q1models} and Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap}
summarise the audit. Mean composite disparity is 0.283 for Qwen2.5-7B, 0.348 for
Mistral-7B, 0.405 for Phi-3.5-mini and 0.517 for Qwen2.5-1.5B. In the Qwen2.5 family the
1.5B model is roughly 1.8 times as disparate as the 7B model; this is the clearest
single pattern in the study, although the paired difference is only suggestive after
correction (median item difference $-0.164$, raw $p=0.0296$, Holm $p=0.177$).

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\input{tables/tab_q1_models.tex}
\caption{Model-level audit summary. Disparity $z$ is the mean of the 20 item-level
composite scores; mean $|\Delta s|$ is the mean absolute sentiment difference on the
0--1 probability scale; repeat consistency is the share of item--seed comparisons that
agree in sign with the majority; refusal rate is the share of baseline responses that
decline. ``Largest dimension'' is the dimension with the highest mean $z$ for that
model.}
\label{tab:q1models}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.86\textwidth]{fig1_dimension_model_heatmap.png}
\caption{Composite disparity $z$ by dimension and model. Rows are the ten dimensions,
columns the four models. Darker cells indicate larger normalised differences between
the two counterfactual conditions. Language/code-switching and religious accommodation
are the two hottest rows; socioeconomic status is dominated by the 1.5B model.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Dimension structure.} Disparities are not uniform
(Table~\ref{tab:q1dims}). The largest average effects are religious accommodation
(0.602), language/code-switching (0.578) and socioeconomic status (0.459). The smallest
are gender and professional roles (0.162) and regional representation (0.278). This
ordering matters: gender is the most heavily benchmarked dimension and is also the one
where these models look comparatively even-handed, whereas accommodation and
language-mixing decisions --- rarely covered by benchmarks --- show the largest gaps.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\input{tables/tab_q1_dims.tex}
\caption{Mean composite disparity $z$ for each dimension and model. The final column is
the unweighted mean over the four models. Values are computed from seed-averaged
item-level deltas.}
\label{tab:q1dims}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{fig2_item_forest.png}
\caption{Item-level mean sentiment deltas with 95\% item-bootstrap confidence intervals.
The intervals mostly straddle zero and the signs differ across items: pooled signed
sentiment is not a useful bias statistic here. The absolute magnitudes, which enter the
composite index, are non-zero.}
\label{fig:forest}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Signed sentiment is null; substance is not.} Mean absolute sentiment
differences are 0.071--0.093, but signed differences are indistinguishable from zero for
every model (all Holm-adjusted $p=1.0$; Figure~\ref{fig:forest}). This is not a
contradiction: the sign of the difference varies across items, so the mean cancels. The
disparity lives in \emph{what} the model provides --- how many words, how many concrete
recommendations, whether a request is approved --- rather than in uniformly warmer or
colder language. An audit that only measured sentiment would have concluded that these
models are unbiased.

\paragraph{Directive hypotheses are often reversed.} Exactly 7 of 20 directional
hypotheses are supported for each model (Table~\ref{tab:q1hyp}). The equality across
models is coincidental, but the low rate is not: 29 of the 80 model--item deltas are
exactly zero (the favourability parser found no explicit decision) and 3 are undefined,
leaving 48 informative deltas, of which 28 (58\%) follow the pre-specified direction ---
consistent with chance (two-sided binomial $p\approx0.31$). Several of the largest effects
run \emph{against} the conventional stereotype. For example, Phi-3.5-mini approves a
recurring Friday-prayer break but declines the identical request framed as a gym session
as the quote below shows; Mistral-7B does the reverse. This is why the design fixes the
hypothesis direction in advance and reports reverse disparities separately rather than
assuming a single ``disadvantaged group''.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\input{tables/tab_q1_hyp.tex}
\caption{Directional hypothesis support. ``Supported'' counts items whose mean delta has
the pre-specified sign; ``all three seeds'' additionally requires that sign in every
seed. No significance threshold is applied here, so these counts are descriptive.}
\label{tab:q1hyp}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig3_sentiment_delta.png}
\caption{Sentiment deltas by dimension.}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig5_length.png}
\caption{Response-length deltas by item.}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Two views of the same audit. Sentiment differences are small and
sign-inconsistent (left); length differences are larger and systematically favour one
condition within an item (right), especially for L1/L2 (language) and S1 (school type).}
\label{fig:two_views}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Qualitative evidence.} The aggregate numbers are easier to interpret with
concrete pairs. In W1, the only difference is the reason for the
break:
\begin{quote}\small
\emph{Variant A (Friday prayers):} ``I have approved your recurring 30-minute break at
1\,pm on Fridays.''\\
\emph{Variant B (gym session):} ``Our current policy only permits scheduled breaks \dots\
I encourage you to use your lunch hour for a gym session instead.''
\end{quote}
The qualifications are identical in the prompt; only the justification changes. The
model treats religious observance as a protected accommodation and exercise as a
discretionary request. In Mistral the pattern is inverted: the gym request is granted
more warmly than the prayer request. In L1, Phi-3.5-mini writes 87 more words in English
than in Hinglish for the same six-week study plan, and includes more named courses and
resources; in S1 the 1.5B model recommends more concrete colleges and scholarships to the
urban-private-school student than to the rural-government-school student. These are
allocational differences with practical consequences even when tone is neutral.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{fig4_refusal.png}
\caption{Refusal rates by model and dimension. Refusals are essentially absent (one
response in 480, from the 1.5B model), so refusal-rate disparity is not a useful
indicator for this prompt set. We report it as a null result rather than dropping it.}
\label{fig:refusal}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Null results: stereotype lexicon and refusal.} The curated stereotype rate is
0.000 for every model and dimension, and the refusal rate is 0.000 for the three larger
models and 0.008 for Qwen2.5-1.5B. Two interpretations are possible: either the models
avoid overt stereotype vocabulary and refusal, or the lexicons are too coarse to catch
modern, polite bias. The qualitative examples support the second reading. Modern
instruction tuning appears to have removed the \emph{surface} markers that older audits
measured while leaving \emph{allocational} differences intact. This is an important
negative result for the assignment's ``stereotype prevalence'' indicator: on this prompt
set it has no variance and therefore cannot discriminate between models.

\paragraph{Statistical significance.} After Holm correction across the seven composite
indicators, no model--metric combination reaches $p<0.05$. The closest is Qwen2.5-7B's
response length (raw $p=0.046$, Holm $p=0.503$). The Friedman test across the four models
gives $\chi^2=7.02$, $p=0.071$; pairwise comparisons are all non-significant after Holm.
We therefore describe the ordering as suggestive, not established. The correct
conclusion is not ``there is no bias'' but ``the effects are small relative to
seed-to-seed variance at this sample size, and the study is underpowered for
model-ranking claims''. The item-level effect sizes and the qualitative reversals are more
informative than the global $p$-values.

\subsection{Mitigation experiments}

We selected the eight items with the largest baseline composite disparity (S1, W1, M2,
L1, L2, W2, N2, C2) and evaluated two interventions on the three larger models, with two
seeds and both variants (192 responses):

\begin{description}[leftmargin=1.2em,style=nextline]
\item[I1 --- fairness system prompt.] A short instruction to apply identical standards
to every person and place, prepended as a system message. It is cheap and does not change
the user request.
\item[I2 --- counterfactual self-audit rewrite.] The model first answers normally, then
is shown its own answer with an instruction to find assumptions that would change if the
manipulated attribute were different and to rewrite symmetrically. It costs a second
generation and therefore roughly doubles latency and compute.
\end{description}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\input{tables/tab_q1_mit.tex}
\caption{Mitigation results on the eight highest-disparity items. $p$ is a paired
Wilcoxon test over the eight item-level composite scores (intervention vs.\ baseline);
with $n=8$ the smallest attainable $p$ is 0.008. ``Words'' is the mean response length
under the intervention.}
\label{tab:q1mit}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig6_mitigation.png}
\caption{Disparity before and after the two interventions. Neither intervention produces
a consistent reduction across models; the largest nominal improvement (Phi-3.5, I1,
0.718$\to$0.532) does not survive correction, and Mistral-7B becomes worse under I1.}
\label{fig:mit}
\end{figure}

Neither intervention reduces disparity reliably (Table~\ref{tab:q1mit},
Figure~\ref{fig:mit}). I1 leaves Qwen2.5-7B essentially unchanged (0.380$\to$0.375,
$p=0.945$), nominally improves Phi-3.5-mini (0.718$\to$0.532, $p=0.078$) and makes
Mistral-7B worse (0.499$\to$0.677, $p=0.312$). I2 helps Mistral and Phi numerically but
not significantly, and hurts Qwen. Answer quality is preserved under both interventions:
refusal is zero, stereotype rate is zero, and mean length and specificity remain in the
same range as the baseline. The failure is therefore not a quality trade-off; the
interventions simply do not address the mechanism that produces the differences.

This result is consistent with the ``moral self-correction'' literature
\citep{ganguli2023selfcorrection}: instructions can change stated values without
reliably changing behaviour on concrete allocation tasks. A more promising direction is
training-time alignment (preference optimisation on paired outputs) or constrained
decoding that enforces parity of specificity, but evaluating either exceeds the scope of
this assignment.

\subsection{Threats to validity}
\label{sec:q1limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sample size.} Twenty items per model give limited power. The composite
      index is precise enough for descriptive comparison but not for strong ranking
      claims. More items per dimension, not more seeds per item, would be the right
      investment.
\item \textbf{Annotator validity.} Sentiment and lexicons are proxies. Favourability
      parsers are item-specific and return zero when the model does not make an explicit
      decision, which inflates the number of null deltas. A human validation subset would
      strengthen the measurement.
\item \textbf{Model coverage.} Four open-weight models, three families, English or
      transliterated Hindi. Results should not be generalised to frontier proprietary
      models or to other languages.
\item \textbf{Prompt sensitivity.} A single template per item cannot rule out
      phrase-level artefacts. The three-seed design captures decoding noise but not
      template sensitivity; paraphrasing each item would address this.
\item \textbf{Multiple comparisons.} We correct within the seven composite indicators and
      across pairwise model tests, but not across every exploratory figure. The
      uncorrected item-level patterns should be read as hypothesis-generating.
\end{itemize}

% ======================================================================================
\section{Question 2: Federated averaging versus centralized training}
% ======================================================================================

\subsection{Setup}

We use Fashion-MNIST with a stratified balanced subset: 1{,}200 training images per class
(12{,}000 total) and 200 test images per class (2{,}000 total). Pixels are normalised with
the dataset mean and standard deviation (0.2860, 0.3530). The model is a small CNN with
two $3\times3$ convolution blocks (1$\to$32, 32$\to$64, each followed by ReLU and
$2\times2$ max-pooling), a 128-unit fully connected layer and a 10-way output, for
421{,}642 parameters. There is no BatchNorm: the same architecture is used in Question~3,
where BatchNorm would break the per-example gradient computation and the formal privacy
accounting.

Federated training runs for 20 rounds with five clients, two local epochs per round,
batch size 64, SGD with momentum 0.9, learning rate 0.01 and weight decay
$5\times10^{-4}$. The centralized baseline uses the same optimizer, batch size and
learning rate for 40 epochs on the union of the training data, so both methods see the
same number of passes (20 rounds $\times$ 2 local epochs $=40$). The test set is a common
held-out set for every method.

\subsection{Partitions}

Four partitions isolate different sources of heterogeneity:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item \textbf{IID}: examples shuffled uniformly across five equal clients.
\item \textbf{Dirichlet($\alpha=0.5$)}: class-wise Dirichlet allocation, moderate label
      imbalance.
\item \textbf{Dirichlet($\alpha=0.1$)}: strongly skewed label distributions.
\item \textbf{Label skew}: each client receives only two classes, the extreme case in
      which no client ever sees eight of the ten labels.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Algorithm}

The server initialises $\theta^0$ and, in each round $t$, broadcasts the global
parameters to every client. Client $k$ runs $E$ local SGD epochs on its shard
$\mathcal{D}_k$ and returns $\theta_k^t$. The server computes the size-weighted average
\[
\theta^{t+1} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \frac{n_k}{n}\,\theta_k^t,
\qquad n=\sum_{k=1}^{K} n_k,
\]
and evaluates the global model on the shared test set. This is the canonical FedAvg of
\citet{mcmahan2017fedavg}. We implement the aggregation explicitly (stacking the client
state dictionaries and taking a weighted sum) rather than using a federated-learning
library, so the averaging step is fully visible.

\subsection{Results}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\small
\input{tables/tab_q2_runs.tex}
\caption{Federated versus centralized results. ``Gap vs C.'' is centralized minus the
method's final accuracy (positive means the federated model is worse). ``Rounds to 90\%''
is the first round reaching 90\% of that run's final accuracy. ``Client mean/std/min'' is
the accuracy of the global model on the test examples whose labels a client owns.
Communication is the total bytes transferred: model updates for FedAvg
($2\times$parameters$\times4$ bytes$\times5$ clients$\times20$ rounds) and one upload of
the raw training set for centralized training.}
\label{tab:q2runs}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig_q2_curves.png}
\caption{Test accuracy per communication round. IID tracks centralized training almost
exactly. Dirichlet($\alpha{=}0.1$) converges more slowly and to a lower plateau; the
two-class label skew peaks early and then oscillates as clients pull the global model
towards their own labels.}
\label{fig:q2curves}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{IID: federation is almost free.} FedAvg reaches 0.885 against 0.889 for
centralized training, a gap of 0.4 percentage points, and matches the centralized
trajectory from the first few rounds (Figure~\ref{fig:q2curves}). With five clients and
identical data distributions, averaging introduces little bias; the small residual gap is
consistent with the extra stochasticity of periodic averaging and with the fact that each
round begins from a shared model rather than a continually updated one.

\paragraph{Moderate heterogeneity: a small, stable cost.} Dirichlet($\alpha=0.5$) reaches
0.855 (gap 3.5 points). Convergence speed is essentially unchanged (three rounds to 90\%
of final), so the cost appears as a lower plateau rather than slower learning. This is the
regime in which FedAvg is usually deployed, and the cost is modest.

\paragraph{Strong heterogeneity: slower and lower.} Dirichlet($\alpha=0.1$) reaches 0.831
(gap 5.8 points) and needs ten rounds to reach 90\% of its final accuracy, more than three
times as long as IID. Per-client accuracy is uneven (mean 0.814, standard deviation 0.027,
minimum 0.780), because the global model is better on classes that are well represented
across clients. Client drift --- local updates moving towards each client's own label
distribution --- is the mechanism; server-side averaging only partially corrects it.

\paragraph{Extreme label skew: the global model fails on some clients.} With two classes
per client, final accuracy is 0.612 and best accuracy 0.707, a gap of 27.8 points. The
per-client spread is dramatic: mean 0.612, standard deviation 0.329, minimum 0.035. The
global model is essentially unusable for the classes owned by the worst client, even
though the same classes are learned well by the clients that own them. The aggregate
accuracy also hides this: a model can look mediocre overall while being catastrophic for a
subgroup. This is the fairness argument for reporting per-client metrics, not just a
single test number.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_q2_client_acc.png}
\caption{Per-client accuracy under each partition.}
\end{subfigure}\hfill
\begin{subfigure}{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_q2_partition.png}
\caption{Class distribution for Dirichlet($\alpha=0.5$).}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Left: the global model's accuracy on the test examples whose labels each client
owns. The two-class skew produces near-zero accuracy for one client while others remain
above 0.9. Right: an example of the label imbalance induced by Dirichlet allocation.}
\label{fig:q2client}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Communication.} FedAvg transfers 337.3\,MB in total (two directions, five
clients, twenty rounds, 421{,}642 parameters at four bytes each, plus the broadcast).
Centralized training transfers the raw training set once: 12{,}000 images at
$28\times28$ float32, or 37.6\,MB. In this small-scale experiment federation is therefore
about nine times more expensive in communication. The comparison flips with dataset size:
model size is fixed while the raw dataset grows, so for large image corpora federation
can be cheaper. The relevant point for the assignment is that ``data never leaves the
client'' is not the same as ``communication is free''.

\subsection{What privacy claims can and cannot be made}

FedAvg keeps raw examples on the clients, which is a genuine data-minimisation benefit:
the server never sees the images. It does \emph{not} provide a formal privacy guarantee.
The server observes each client's parameter update, and updates are known to leak
information about their training data through gradient inversion, property inference and
membership inference \citep{zhu2019deep,shokri2017membership}. A malicious server can
also manipulate the global model to probe a client. The following claims would therefore
be unsupported by this implementation:
\begin{itemize}
\item ``FedAvg guarantees differential privacy.'' It does not; no noise is added.
\item ``The server cannot learn anything about client data.'' It learns a high-dimensional
      function of that data.
\item ``Clients are protected from each other.'' They are, in the sense that they never
      exchange data, but the shared model encodes information from all clients.
\end{itemize}
Legitimate claims are narrower: raw data stays local, the server sees only updates, and
the system can be composed with secure aggregation \citep{bonawitz2019towards} and
client-level DP to obtain formal guarantees. Question~3 implements the example-level
DP mechanism that would be needed on the client side.

\subsection{Limitations}

The subset is deliberately small so that many configurations can be run; absolute
accuracies are lower than full Fashion-MNIST results and the gaps should be read
relatively. We simulate full participation (all five clients every round) and do not model
stragglers, dropouts, client sampling or heterogeneous compute. Per-client accuracy is
computed on the global test set restricted to the labels a client owns, which measures
group-level fairness rather than each client's local held-out performance. Finally, the
comparison uses one architecture and one optimizer; FedProx
\citep{li2020fedprox} or server momentum would narrow the heterogeneity gap, but that is
outside the assignment's scope.

% ======================================================================================
\section{Question 3: Differentially private CNN training}
% ======================================================================================

\subsection{DP-SGD implementation}

We implement DP-SGD \citep{abadi2016dpsgd} from scratch. For a batch of $B$ examples, the
per-example gradients are computed with \texttt{torch.func.vmap} over a functional call
to the model, giving one gradient tensor per parameter per example. Each example gradient
is clipped,
\[
\bar{g}_i = g_i \cdot \min\!\left(1, \frac{C}{\|g_i\|_2}\right),
\]
and the batch update is
\[
\tilde{g} = \frac{1}{B}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{B}\bar{g}_i
             + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 C^2 I)\right).
\]
The noise is added to the sum and the result divided by $B$, which is algebraically the
same as adding $\mathcal{N}(0,(\sigma C/B)^2 I)$ to the mean. We use SGD with momentum
0.9, learning rate 0.1, batch size 256 and no weight decay. Momentum is applied after the
noisy gradient; this is the standard ``noisy SGD with momentum'' variant and is not
itself accounted for in the privacy analysis, a point we return to below.

BatchNorm is absent by design. Per-example gradients through BatchNorm depend on the
other examples in the batch, so the per-example clipping bound is not well defined and the
formal guarantee is broken. Using GroupNorm or no normalisation is the usual fix; we use
no normalisation and keep the architecture identical to Question~2.

\subsection{Privacy accounting}

The sampling rate is $q=B/N=256/12{,}000=0.0213$ and one epoch has
$\lfloor 12{,}000/256\rfloor=46$ steps, so 15 epochs give 690 steps. We compute
$(\eps,\delta)$ with $\delta=10^{-5}$ using two accountants: the RDP accountant
\citep{mironov2017rdp} and the PRV accountant \citep{gopi2021prv}, both from Opacus. The
PRV numbers are consistently lower and are the ones we quote when a single value is
needed. The accountant assumes Poisson subsampling; our loader uses fixed-size shuffled
batches without replacement, which is the usual approximation and is slightly
conservative in practice.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\footnotesize
\input{tables/tab_q3_runs.tex}
\caption{DP-SGD runs. $C$ is the clipping norm, $\sigma$ the noise multiplier,
$\eps_{\mathrm{RDP}}$ and $\eps_{\mathrm{PRV}}$ the privacy budget at $\delta=10^{-5}$,
``clipped frac.'' the fraction of examples whose gradient exceeded $C$. The baseline is
non-private. The three ablation rows use $C=1,\sigma=0.8$; the final row is the overfit
baseline used only for the membership-inference experiment.}
\label{tab:q3runs}
\end{table}

\subsection{Utility and convergence}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{fig_q3_acc_vs_eps.png}
\caption{Test accuracy against the achieved privacy budget. Each point is one
configuration; lower $\eps$ means stronger privacy. The dashed and dotted lines are the
two non-private baselines.}
\label{fig:q3eps}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig_q3_curves.png}
\caption{Test accuracy per epoch for every configuration. Noise slows convergence and
lowers the plateau; the $C=5,\sigma=1.2$ run is the clearest example of noise-dominated
training.}
\label{fig:q3curves}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Noise dominates utility.} At $C=1$, accuracy falls from 0.879 (non-private) to
0.817 at $\sigma=0.4$ ($\eps=47.8$), 0.812 at $\sigma=0.8$ ($\eps=6.5$) and 0.795 at
$\sigma=1.2$ ($\eps=2.7$). The accuracy cost is front-loaded: the first step to
$\sigma=0.4$ accounts for 6.2 of the 8.4 points lost, yet $\eps$ is still 47.8. The much
larger privacy gain from 47.8 to 6.5 costs only another 0.5 points, and the final step to
2.7 costs 1.7 points. At $C=5$ the trade-off is much steeper: 0.824 at $\sigma=0.4$, 0.748 at
$\sigma=0.8$, and 0.636 at $\sigma=1.2$. The reason is visible in the mean gradient norm:
at $C=5,\sigma=1.2$ the average per-example norm is 131.8, so most of the gradient is
discarded by clipping and the remaining signal is buried in noise.

\paragraph{Clipping: smaller is not always better.} $C=1$ clips about 47\% of examples at
$\sigma=0.4$ and $C=5$ about 31\%. Despite clipping more aggressively, $C=1$ is only marginally worse
at $\sigma=0.4$ (0.817 vs 0.824) and much better at high noise (0.795 vs 0.636 at $\sigma=1.2$). The reason is the interaction with
noise: the noise scale is $\sigma C$, so a larger $C$ multiplies the noise added to every
coordinate. With a small $C$, per-example contributions are bounded tightly and the
signal-to-noise ratio is better even though more examples are clipped. The optimal $C$
therefore depends on $\sigma$, and tuning it jointly is more important than tuning either
parameter alone.

\paragraph{Training duration.} At $C=1,\sigma=0.8$, five epochs give 0.768 at $\eps=4.3$;
fifteen epochs give 0.812 at $\eps=6.5$; thirty epochs give 0.821 at $\eps=9.0$. Longer
training improves utility but spends privacy budget linearly in the number of steps. The
marginal return is small: doubling from 15 to 30 epochs buys 0.9 points for 38\% more
$\eps$. This is the expected behaviour when the model is capacity-limited rather than
data-limited.

\paragraph{Dataset size.} Halving the training set to 6{,}000 examples reduces accuracy
from 0.812 to 0.774 at the same $\sigma$, while the privacy budget rises from 6.5 to 9.5
because the sampling rate doubles (fewer examples, same batch size) and the number of
steps halves. The accuracy loss reflects less data; the budget increase reflects the
larger per-step sampling fraction. The two effects are often conflated in informal
discussions of ``small datasets are more private''; in DP-SGD the sampling rate matters
more than the raw count.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{fig_q3_ablation.png}
\caption{Ablations at $C=1,\sigma=0.8$. Left: training duration, with the achieved $\eps$
on the secondary axis. Right: dataset size. Both change utility and privacy at the same
time, so they must be reported together.}
\label{fig:q3abl}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Membership inference}

\paragraph{Attacks.} We evaluate four attacks. Three are threshold attacks on a single
per-example statistic: negative loss, maximum softmax confidence, and negative predictive
entropy; for each we report ROC-AUC, TPR at 1\% and 10\% FPR, and advantage over random
guessing at 10\% FPR. The fourth is a shadow-model attack
\citep{shokri2017membership}: four non-private shadow models are trained on random halves
of the training set, their per-example (loss, confidence, entropy, correctness) features
are labelled member/non-member, and a logistic regression is fitted and applied to the
target model's features. Members are the 12{,}000 training examples; non-members are the
2{,}000 test examples. The shadow models are intentionally non-private, so the attack is
given a favourable setting.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\footnotesize
\input{tables/tab_q3_mia.tex}
\caption{Membership-inference results. AUC 0.5 is random guessing. The non-private
baseline barely leaks; the deliberately overfit baseline leaks more, and every DP
configuration returns to chance. TPR at 10\% FPR is close to 0.10 for all models.}
\label{tab:q3mia}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{fig_q3_mia.png}
\caption{Left: membership-inference ROC-AUC against $\eps$ for the loss and shadow
attacks, with the overfit non-private baseline as a dotted line. Right: shadow-attack
advantage at 10\% FPR. The DP points sit on the 0.5 line; the non-private baselines sit
above it.}
\label{fig:q3mia}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Results.} The standard 15-epoch non-private baseline is already hard to attack:
loss AUC 0.532, shadow AUC 0.560, shadow advantage at 10\% FPR 0.016. It overfits little
(train and test accuracy are both close to 0.88), so there is little membership signal.
Every DP configuration is at chance: loss AUC 0.499--0.513, shadow AUC 0.500--0.518,
advantage between $-0.006$ and $+0.002$. The overfit baseline, trained for 30 epochs with
no weight decay, reaches 0.972 training and 0.865 test accuracy and gives shadow AUC
0.579 --- a small but measurable signal. DP at $\eps=6.5$ reduces the same attack to
0.509. This is the cleanest demonstration in the question: the privacy benefit of DP is
largest exactly when the non-private model overfits, and it is invisible when the baseline
already generalises.

\paragraph{What the attack does not show.} A membership AUC near 0.5 is evidence that
\emph{this} attack fails, not proof that no attack can succeed. Shadow models are small
and trained on half the data; the attack sees only four summary statistics; and the target
and shadow distributions are the same. A stronger attack (many shadows, per-class
calibration, likelihood-ratio attacks) could find signal that ours misses. The formal
$(\eps,\delta)$ guarantee is the stronger statement, and it holds independently of the
attack. Conversely, the DP guarantee is only as good as its accounting assumptions: we
use fixed-size batches instead of Poisson sampling, momentum is outside the accountant,
and the clipping bound is enforced per example but the privacy analysis assumes the
worst case over all neighbouring datasets. These are standard practical caveats, and they
mean the reported $\eps$ should be read as an optimistic estimate of the guarantee.

\subsection{Discussion}

The three questions connect in a useful way. Question~1 shows that bias in modern
instruction-tuned models is often allocational and subtle, not a matter of overtly
stereotyped words. Question~2 shows that decentralised training can match centralised
accuracy under IID data but distributes errors very unevenly under label skew, so
aggregate metrics hide harm to specific clients. Question~3 shows that DP-SGD's utility
cost is governed by the noise-to-clip ratio $\sigma C$ and by the number of steps, and
that empirical privacy attacks can under-report leakage when the model does not overfit.
In all three cases the methodological lesson is the same: the headline aggregate is not
the finding. Dimension-level, client-level and per-example analyses are what make the
results interpretable.

% ======================================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ======================================================================================

We built and executed three reproducible pipelines. The bias audit finds composite
disparities of 0.28--0.52 pooled standard deviations across four models and ten
dimensions, with the strongest effects in language mixing, religious accommodation and
socioeconomic status; the effects are allocational rather than tonal, and the smallest
model in a matched family is the most disparate. Two inference-time mitigations fail to
reduce disparity reliably. FedAvg matches centralized training within 0.4 points under
IID data, loses 3.5--5.8 points under Dirichlet heterogeneity, and loses 27.8 points under
extreme label skew, with per-client accuracy collapsing to 0.035 for the worst client.
DP-SGD at $\eps\approx6.5$ retains 0.812 accuracy at $C=1$ (7 points below
non-private), while $C=5$ at the same noise retains only 0.748; membership inference is at
chance for every DP configuration and measurably above chance for an overfit non-private
baseline. The code, prompts, responses, annotations and result tables are submitted
alongside this report.

